from report_builder_utils import add_styled_heading, add_p, add_bullet

def build_chapter_1(doc):
    add_styled_heading(doc, "CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION", level=1)
    
    add_styled_heading(doc, "1.1 Background and Agricultural Context", level=2)
    add_p(doc, "Agriculture is the foundational pillar of human civilization, global food security, rural economic vitality, and sustainable ecosystem management. Throughout human history, the cultivation of crops has shaped demographic distribution, economic trade routes, and geopolitical stability. In the modern era, agriculture faces an unprecedented convergence of global pressures. The global human population is projected by the United Nations to approach 9.8 billion by 2050 and surpass 10.4 billion by the end of the century. Meeting the nutritional and calorific requirements of this expanding populace necessitates a projected 60% to 70% increase in aggregate agricultural output. However, this production surge must be achieved under the strict confines of planetary boundaries: arable land reserves are virtually exhausted, freshwater aquifers are being depleted at rates far exceeding natural replenishment, soil degradation and salinization affect over 30% of global agricultural soils, and anthropogenic climate change introduces extreme meteorological volatility.")
    
    add_p(doc, "Historically, agricultural decision-making—and most critically, the foundational choice of which crop cultivar to sow at the onset of a planting season—has relied on generational empirical heuristics, localized folklore, and rudimentary regional calendars. While traditional farming knowledge reflects valuable long-term adaptations to historical macro-climates, it possesses intrinsic limitations when confronted with rapid environmental shifts. Traditional heuristics assume static climatic patterns and stable soil chemistry. Today, global warming has disrupted traditional monsoon onsets, prolonged intra-seasonal droughts, increased vapor pressure deficits, and altered soil nutrient mineralization rates. When smallholder farmers select crops based on obsolete seasonal assumptions rather than quantifiable, real-time agro-ecological measurements, the consequences are severe: widespread crop failure, wasted synthetic fertilizer inputs, irreversible topsoil acidification, and devastating financial bankruptcy that threatens rural community survival.")

    add_p(doc, "The emergence of Precision Agriculture (PA) and Smart Farming—often conceptualized as the core of the fourth agricultural revolution, or Agriculture 4.0—offers a transformative pathway out of this systemic vulnerability. Precision agriculture seeks to replace subjective intuition with empirical, site-specific, data-driven optimization. By treating farming fields not as homogeneous land units, but as heterogeneous, dynamic biochemical systems, precision farming matches biological crop requirements with localized soil and climatic conditions. The rapid proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) sensor nodes, localized automated agro-meteorological stations, and digital soil testing kits has made it feasible to capture high-resolution agronomic telemetry at field scale. Specifically, agricultural decision-support systems evaluate seven vital physiological indicators:")

    add_bullet(doc, "Soil Nitrogen (N): ", "As the primary chemical building block of amino acids, proteins, and chlorophyll molecules, nitrogen governs vegetative vigor, leaf canopy expansion, and light interception efficiency. Deficiencies cause chlorosis and stunted biomass, whereas excessive application delays physiological maturation, increases lodging vulnerability, and produces nitrogen oxide leaching.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Soil Phosphorus (P): ", "Phosphorus is fundamental to cellular energy storage and transfer through adenosine triphosphate (ATP), nucleic acid synthesis, root architectural development, and early seedling establishment. It dictates flower induction, seed formation, and reproductive yield.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Soil Potassium (K): ", "Potassium functions as an enzymatic catalyst and osmotic regulator, maintaining cellular turgor, opening and closing stomatal apertures, regulating plant water-use efficiency, and synthesizing starches. It confers biological resistance against pest infestation, pathogen invasion, and cold or drought stress.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Ambient Temperature: ", "Ambient thermal energy dictates biochemical reaction kinetics, enzymatic denaturation thresholds, cellular respiration rates, and growing degree day (GDD) accumulation. Every plant species exhibits distinct minimum, optimum, and maximum cardinal temperature boundaries.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Relative Atmospheric Humidity: ", "Atmospheric humidity controls the atmospheric vapor pressure deficit (VPD) between the internal leaf stomatal cavity and ambient air. Low humidity induces excessive transpiration and cellular dehydration, while prolonged saturation inhibits cooling and promotes fungal spores.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Soil Acidity (pH): ", "Soil pH governs the chemical solubility and biological bioavailability of essential nutrients. Strongly acidic soils (pH < 5.5) induce aluminum and manganese toxicities while locking phosphorus into insoluble complexes; alkaline soils (pH > 8.0) precipitate micronutrients like iron, zinc, and boron into inaccessible carbonates.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Seasonal Rainfall / Precipitation: ", "Precipitation provides the essential solvent for mineral dissolution and root nutrient transport. Rainfall quantity, distribution, and seasonal timing determine soil moisture regimes, aeration status, and the fundamental viability of rainfed farming systems.")

    add_p(doc, "In recent years, the intersection of precision agriculture and Artificial Intelligence (AI) has established automated crop recommendation as a vital predictive modeling frontier. By training supervised Machine Learning (ML) and Deep Learning (DL) models on historical soil and climate records, algorithms can learn multi-dimensional mappings between input parameters and optimal crop species, providing automated advisory guidance to farm operators.")

    add_styled_heading(doc, "1.2 Problem Addressed", level=2)
    add_p(doc, "Despite encouraging accuracy figures reported in academic literature, existing machine learning and deep learning solutions face fundamental conceptual and practical barriers when transitioned from pristine benchmark datasets to unpredictable agricultural fields. Our analysis identifies five critical vulnerabilities in current crop recommendation systems:")

    add_p(doc, "1. The Agnostic Tabular Dilemma (Absence of Agronomic Domain Guidance): The overwhelming majority of machine learning models—spanning Random Forests, Support Vector Machines, Gradient-Boosted Decision Trees (XGBoost, LightGBM), and recent Deep Tabular architectures (FT-Transformer, TabNet)—treat agricultural datasets as abstract numerical matrices. These algorithms optimize unconstrained statistical loss functions purely against empirical patterns present in the training set. However, plant biology does not operate in an unconstrained mathematical void; crop growth is strictly governed by physiological tolerance boundaries and biochemical Liebig laws codified by global institutions like the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and the Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR). When purely data-driven models encounter small, unrepresentative, or slightly shifted datasets, they readily infer spurious statistical artifacts that violate basic agronomic principles, recommending crops in environments where biological survival is impossible.")

    add_p(doc, "2. Opaque Decisions and Lack of Farmer-Facing Explainability: Modern deep neural architectures and complex ensemble classifiers function as opaque, mathematical 'black boxes.' In an agricultural context, selecting a crop cultivar represents a major capital, labor, and temporal investment across an entire 4-to-6 month cultivation cycle. Smallholder farmers and professional agronomists cannot blindly accept an unsubstantiated crop label emitted by an automated algorithm. If a system recommends chickpea over rice, it must provide verifiable, intuitive explanations showing which nutrient concentrations (e.g., high phosphorus sensitivity) or climatic parameters (e.g., restricted rainfall tolerance) drove that decision. Existing systems almost universally fail to deliver faithful, multi-perspective feature attributions.")

    add_p(doc, "3. Brittleness Under Sensor Noise, Missing Data, and Environmental Shifts: Real agricultural fields are hostile operating environments for electronics. In-situ IoT soil probes and telemetry stations experience hardware degradation, battery exhaustion, physical electrode corrosion, transmission packet loss, and calibration drift. Furthermore, micro-climates regularly exhibit seasonal anomalies that shift measurements outside historical training distributions. While standard algorithms perform well on clean, pre-sanitized test sets, their accuracy collapses when evaluated against missing sensor readings or noisy telemetry. Tree-based models cannot extrapolate beyond axis-aligned training splits, while deep transformers suffer internal representation collapse when inputs are partially masked.")

    add_p(doc, "4. Overconfident Predictions and Calibration Blindness: Standard neural network classifiers utilize softmax output layers that normalize raw logits into pseudo-probabilities. However, it is well-established in machine learning theory that modern deep neural networks are notoriously poorly calibrated—they frequently emit 99% confidence scores on inputs that are severely corrupted or entirely out-of-distribution. In agricultural applications, a deterministic classifier that cannot quantify its own epistemic uncertainty poses substantial financial danger. An automated advisory tool must have the introspective capability to say 'I am uncertain' and trigger an alert for manual soil laboratory testing when conditions are anomalous.")

    add_p(doc, "5. Excessive Computational Overhead for Rural Edge Hardware: Advanced deep learning models, particularly tabular attention networks, possess multi-million parameter footprints and require substantial floating-point operations. In rural farming communities across developing regions, cloud connectivity is frequently intermittent or unavailable, forcing reliance on low-cost smartphones or battery-powered edge microcontrollers. Executing computationally heavy neural network forward passes with latencies exceeding 100 milliseconds for routine, unambiguous queries wastes scarce electrical power and impedes responsive interactive adoption.")

    add_styled_heading(doc, "1.3 Motivation", level=2)
    add_p(doc, "The motivation behind this project originates from the urgent need to bridge the profound divide between purely statistical machine learning and established biological agronomic science. While computer science has rapidly accelerated model complexity, agriculture demands reliability, interpretability, and practical field robustness.")

    add_p(doc, "From an architectural perspective, this research is motivated by the complementary nature of distinct machine learning paradigms. Tree-based ensemble algorithms (most notably XGBoost) represent the undisputed benchmark for tabular classification because their hierarchical orthogonal splits naturally isolate localized, non-linear decision regions and threshold steps. Conversely, Deep Attention Transformers (such as FT-Transformer) project discrete tabular variables into continuous latent spaces, allowing self-attention mechanisms to model global, all-to-all cross-feature interactions. Rather than forcing a false dichotomy between tree models and neural transformers, our goal is to design a unified hybrid framework that synthesizes the localized decision-partitioning strength of XGBoost leaf embeddings with the contextual attention dynamics of transformers, all bound together under the governing constraint of an explicit biological knowledge matrix.")

    add_p(doc, "From a socio-economic perspective, this project is motivated by the plight of smallholder farmers who operate at the razor-thin margin between seasonal profit and destitution. Transforming AI from an academic novelty into a dependable farming asset requires embedding safety, uncertainty awareness, and fault tolerance into every layer of the computation graph. Developing an intelligent system that remains accurate even when sensors fail, explains its reasoning transparently, flags uncertain predictions, and executes in milliseconds on low-power rural hardware provides a direct pathway toward resilient, climate-smart agriculture.")

    add_styled_heading(doc, "1.4 Scope and Objectives of the Project", level=2)
    add_p(doc, "The primary objective of this project is to research, formulate, implement, and benchmark KG-Hybrid—a publication-grade, Knowledge-Guided Trustworthy AI Framework for robust agricultural crop recommendation.")

    add_p(doc, "Specific technical objectives include:", bold_prefix="Technical Objectives: ")
    add_bullet(doc, "1. Formalize Agronomic Knowledge Matrix: ", "Construct a structured, normalized 22 × 7 suitability matrix K derived from official FAO and ICAR ecological crop compendiums, quantifying the physiological tolerance envelopes of 22 candidate crops across 7 environmental features.")
    add_bullet(doc, "2. Architect the KGAAN Core Model: ", "Design and implement the Knowledge-Guided Agriculture-Aware Attention Network, combining an EmbeddingBag projection of frozen 200-tree XGBoost leaf node trajectories with a 4-layer, 8-head multi-head self-attention FT-Transformer.")
    add_bullet(doc, "3. Engineer Gated Knowledge Fusion: ", "Formulate a dynamic gated fusion module incorporating adaptive channel gating, stop-gradient regularization, and projected knowledge bias injection to align neural latent representations with agronomic priors.")
    add_bullet(doc, "4. Implement Multi-Task Regularization: ", "Formulate an end-to-end composite loss function combining task cross-entropy with symmetric knowledge distillation (λ1 = 0.5) and supervised contrastive loss (λ2 = 0.1) across a two-stage training scheme.")
    add_bullet(doc, "5. Conduct Rigorous Comparative Benchmarking: ", "Evaluate KG-Hybrid against six competitive baselines (Logistic Regression, CART, Random Forest, XGBoost, FT-Transformer, Stacking) on a leak-free 70:15:15 stratified split of the 2,200-sample benchmark dataset across multiple metrics (Top-1 Accuracy, Macro-F1, Top-3 Recall).")
    add_bullet(doc, "6. Validate Through Systematic Ablation: ", "Execute a comprehensive 7-variant ablation study (A1 to A7) and multi-seed cross-validation to isolate and quantify the exact performance contribution of each structural sub-module.")
    add_bullet(doc, "7. Perform Robustness Stress Testing: ", "Evaluate model resilience under adverse field perturbations: missing sensor dropout (20%, 40%, 60%) with leak-free KNN (k=5) imputation, electronic Gaussian noise (σ ∈ [0.1, 0.3]), and 1.5× out-of-distribution feature extrapolation.")
    add_bullet(doc, "8. Establish Explainability & Uncertainty Quantification: ", "Extract TreeSHAP feature attributions and attention weights to prove cross-branch biological complementarity (Spearman ρ = 0.43). Integrate Monte Carlo Dropout (M=10) for empirical uncertainty quantification and safety gating.")
    add_bullet(doc, "9. Deliver Production Edge Optimizations: ", "Formulate a confidence-aware two-stage early-exit pipeline achieving a 14× latency reduction, compress the model into a 4.04M-parameter lightweight variant, and deploy the pipeline as an asynchronous FastAPI microservice.")

    add_p(doc, "Scope Boundaries: The experimental scope of this study is grounded on the standard 2,200-sample balanced agricultural crop recommendation benchmark spanning 22 crop classes. The study focuses on multi-class classification from soil and weather telemetry. While physical hardware telemetry deployment and multi-year field trials remain outside the current scope, the computational profiling, ONNX/TFLite export pipelines, and simulated robustness stress-tests establish complete algorithmic readiness for field trials.", bold_prefix="Delimitations & Boundaries: ")

    add_styled_heading(doc, "1.5 Report Organization", level=2)
    add_p(doc, "The remainder of this project report is organized as follows:")
    add_bullet(doc, "Chapter 2 (Literature Review / Existing System): ", "Provides an in-depth critical survey of agricultural decision-support systems, conventional machine learning algorithms, deep tabular transformers, knowledge-guided machine learning, explainable AI, and edge deployment architectures, concluding with identified research gaps and contributions.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Chapter 3 (Proposed Work): ", "Presents the end-to-end methodology of KG-Hybrid, detailing the dataset, preprocessing protocols, agronomic knowledge matrix construction, KGAAN mathematical derivations, multi-task loss formulations, and early-exit cascades.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Chapter 4 (Experimentation and Result Analysis): ", "Documents the experimental setup, baseline comparisons, convergence behavior, ablation studies, sensor degradation stress tests, SHAP explainability, uncertainty quantification, calibration curves, and latency benchmarks.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Chapter 5 (Conclusion and Scope for Further Research): ", "Summarizes primary research findings, critically reflects upon methodological limitations, and outlines future trajectories including satellite multi-modal fusion, knowledge graphs, and embedded microcontroller deployments.")
    add_bullet(doc, "Appendices & References: ", "Provides the implementation directory map, reproducibility checklist, and verified IEEE-formatted references.")

